<header><p class="st-kicker">electorate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Archchancellor's Electorate</h1><p class="st-meta">1803 CE – 1810 CE · capital Regensburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">State created for Karl Theodor von Dalberg, the last Archbishop of Mainz, to keep the imperial archchancellor's office alive.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-mainz">Electorate of Mainz</a> lost its capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Imperial Recess of 1803</a> kept its archbishop, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Karl Theodor von Dalberg</a>, as elector and archchancellor of the Empire, and gave him the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-aschaffenburg">Principality of Aschaffenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-regensburg">Regensburg</a> and the County of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-wetzlar">Wetzlar</a>, with the archiepiscopal seat moved to Regensburg.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Dalberg</a> was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> churchman who governed from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-regensburg">Regensburg</a>, seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Perpetual Imperial Diet</a>. His state was scattered and Catholic, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a> as its richest part. He cultivated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, praising him as a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, and in 1806 became Prince-Primate of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, adding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> to his lands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1810 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Dalberg</a> ceded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-regensburg">Regensburg</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a> and in exchange received <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-hanau-munzenberg">Hanau</a> and Fulda, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-frankfurt">Grand Duchy of Frankfurt</a>. Dalberg remained its ruler until 1813, and Regensburg has belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> ever since.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/archchancellors-electorate.jpg" alt="Archchancellor's Electorate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Principality_of_Regensburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1803 CE – 1810 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>electorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Regensburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Karl Theodor von Dalberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Electorate of Regensburg, Kurerzkanzler</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1806&amp;focus=archchancellors-electorate" aria-label="Archchancellor's Electorate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4959 467 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Archchancellor's Electorate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5279 723L5190 694M5279 723L5229 694M5279 723L5199 691M5279 723L5199 691M5279 723L5279 723M5279 723L5279 723M5279 723L5199 691M5279 723L5279 723M5279 723L5194 678M5279 723L5210 695"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5190 694h0M5229 694h0M5199 691h0M5199 691h0M5279 723h0M5279 723h0M5199 691h0M5279 723h0M5194 678h0M5210 695h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5279 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5279 723h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Regensburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1806&amp;focus=archchancellors-electorate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
